Z||000013||thomas_l||12-17-2001||01:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroSTETZ:<BR><B>it seems to be an issue with my router- unfortunately i cancelled my other ips, or i would just take this one off the router. all the dmz hosting stuff is still in place, as well as ipsec pass through and all that fun crud- let me know if anyone has ideas?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You have to define a default inbound route to your www server on the router, assuming you are using NAT and non-routable private addressing on your home LAN (i.e. 192.168.0.X). Another possibility is that your ISP may block inbound port 80 (many implemented this to reduce Code Red antics). Try setting up the inbound route first and if that doesn't work put your www on a different port.||||reg||1
